1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: In eighteen seventy-four, the English economist William Stanley Jevons printed a puzzle in a book.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: He asked which two numbers, multiplied together, make 8,616,460,799.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: I think it unlikely, he added, that anyone but myself will ever know.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Confident.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Well, multiplying two numbers is easy. Finding them again from the answer can be very hard.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: And lopsided problems like his now guard every secure web page you open.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: They're what let two strangers agree on a secret while everyone listens.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: This is the story of public-key cryptography, and it was invented twice.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: For most of history, sending a secret message meant sharing a secret key first.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: And the same key locks the message and unlocks it, so whoever has it can read everything.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: So the key is the weak point.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: Exactly. It had to travel safely, by trusted courier or at a face-to-face meeting.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: And every pair of people who want to talk privately needs a key of their own.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: Ten people need forty-five keys.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERB: A thousand people need 499,500.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: That's half a million keys to deliver.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERB: Two researchers at Stanford called this the key distribution problem in 1976.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: And their answer sounds impossible. It lets strangers agree on a key in full view of everyone.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: In 1974, Whitfield Diffie, an independent researcher, was advised to meet a young Stanford professor, Martin Hellman.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: They'd planned a half-hour meeting. It lasted many hours.
